# .gitleaksignore -- baseline for the full-history secret scan.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# .github/workflows/secret-history-scan.yml re-reads every reachable commit,
# because the PR-range gate in ci.yml never looks at the past. Its first run
# found 22 matches, all triaged as documentation placeholders, frontend
# identifiers or test fixtures -- and then the job failed, every run, forever.
#
# A guard that is permanently red reports nothing. A 23rd finding, this time a
# live credential, would have looked exactly like the 22: still red, still
# ignored. Pinning the known 22 here is what makes the job able to say
# "something changed".
#
# Why fingerprints and not regexes
# --------------------------------
# .gitleaks.toml already carries line-regex allowlists, each scoped so a real
# key on the same line still fails. That is the right tool for a pattern that
# recurs in new code. It is the wrong tool here: these are fixed points in
# history, and a regex broad enough to cover them could also cover a future
# secret that happens to look similar.
#
# Each entry below is `commit:path:rule:line`. It matches exactly one blob at
# one line of one commit. A new secret is a new commit, so it produces a new
# fingerprint, so it is NOT covered by anything here -- the job goes red and
# stays red until someone looks. That property is what makes a baseline safe;
# warden/tests/test_secret_scan_baseline.py enforces it.
#
# Adding a line to this file is a claim that you opened the commit and read the
# match. Do not paste in a scan's output wholesale.
#
# Baseline set 2026-08-31 from run 33384506909. Reproduced locally against a
# fresh clone of main: 2446 commits, 171.69 MB, 22 findings without this
# file, 0 with it, and 1 with it plus a planted synthetic secret in a new
# commit -- which is the check that it is a baseline and not a blindfold.


